edb/ref 17592186050911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1031 "The Transpose of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1033 232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050727 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6197c039-5830-56a2-b171-eac0f7516ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133597 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133597 #edb/ref 1071 "Verify whether the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\},$ where\n![](images/22/226d83de1fff110426877af083e1aee5eac7f7de1244bb59f7f801aeea8ec6a5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8bbe0e1-8bf1-5b3a-a77e-6350ea77e83f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133597 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we verify whether $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/c0/c051729499bde9389ad7e38ab91d3609c8153cbed59233f4b3e3e7ac9e9c2534.png)\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}26 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133598 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133598 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n![](images/df/df90590ac6801e6f2efaada175e5d8460a45cb06375064eb53f80eddbbd3991c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac8363a8-fc77-52e0-b060-5e3a45c5171a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133598 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A\\left(A^T\\!A\\right)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ In our case, we have\n![](images/a1/a1592d17b242a66e66948692dfaf7892f9df6d415ae96fb59154455ffe1a4ef9.png)\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{32}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133788 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1